WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
All my life, I've dreamed of
getting out there into space.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.560
Now, that was a challenge for me

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.520
because I went
to 13 different schools

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.560
and I have a condition
called dyslexia.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.320
Today I want to talk
about three fellow black scientists,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.520
one from the past,

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.640
one from the present

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.440
and one from the future -

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.600
all pioneers in their field.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.280
My first hero is unfortunately
no longer with us -

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.880
his name was Alan Powell Goffe.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.040
He was born in Kingston upon Thames
in 1920.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.720
His father was Jamaican
and his mother was English.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.640
Both his parents were physicians.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.000
Like me, he was inspired
by his parents

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.760
and gained a degree in medicine
and a diploma in bacteriology.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.840
He went on to specialise in pathology

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.000
the study of the causes and effects
of disease.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.600
In the 1950s, measles was described

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.000
as the greatest
killer of children in history.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.680
Goffe's work on vaccines
was revolutionary

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.400
in ending the measles epidemic.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.640
A sample of the virus
is taken and then weakened

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.480
usually by reproducing it many times.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.720
This creates a living but weakened
version of the original virus

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.720
which the body can fight,
stimulating long-lasting immunity.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.880
He also worked on the polio virus

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.600
and made important contributions

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.560
to the development of a vaccine
for this too.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.680
Tragically, he died young
in a sailing accident

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.600
aged just 46.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.280
Dr Goffe was a true pioneer

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.040
but few people know his name

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.440
and with the important role
that vaccines are playing

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.880
in our lives today

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.680
it's wonderful to celebrate
the work of this fantastic man.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.440
A present-day scientist
who I think you should know

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.640
is Dr Asmeret Asefaw Berhe.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.240
She was born and grew up in Eritrea.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.240
Sadly, when Asmeret was young,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.840
her country was undergoing
a war of independence.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.280
Despite this turmoil,

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.040
she was one of only 1,000 students
admitted to university in Eritrea

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.440
when she left school.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.200
She studied soil science

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.680
and was fascinated
by ecosystems within the soil.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.520
Her childhood experiences
led her to conduct research

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.160
into the effects of landmines
on soil,

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.640
looking at how the explosions
cause disruption

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.240
in the structure of the soil

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.360
and how they can leak toxic chemicals
into the soil.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.200
She also worked out that
if just a quarter of the landmines

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.600
from the affected areas were removed

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.640
that this would enable the land
to produce enough food

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.920
to feed 1.6 million people
every year.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.920
Another brilliant black pioneer.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.920
Her work inspires me
because in my early career

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.280
I worked on the development
of a handheld landmine detector.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.440
also trying to tackle
the same problem

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.080
but from a different angle.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.880
Now, a scientist you should look out
for is Kayisha Payne.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.440
Like me, Kayisha grew up in London

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.600
and showed an early aptitude
for science and maths.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.840
It was chemistry
that most excited her.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.600
She loved the idea of studying

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.280
the fundamental building blocks
of everything.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.400
This passion led her to undertake
a degree in chemical engineering.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.960
She was the first member of her
immediate family to go to university.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.960
Kayisha was a process engineer
at AstraZeneca in Cambridge.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.240
This is where one of the Covid-19
vaccines was developed.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.360
Kayisha's work focused on ensuring
that drugs that were being developed

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:22.000
didn't degrade by the time
they were administered to patients.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.400
Throughout her studies and her career

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.240
she was often
the only black person in the room.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.760
Passionate about having more
diversity in STEM

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.000
Kayisha created the Black British
Professionals In STEM.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.760
A network for black students
in the UK

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:40.120
to connect with their peers and
receive advice and mentoring

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.800
from black British
STEM professionals.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.280
As she says: "When you don't see
yourself represented in an industry

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.160
it's extremely difficult to believe
there's a place for you in it."

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:56.240
These three black scientists
didn't only dream of a better future

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.120
but had the determination
to make it a reality.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.600
I'm truly inspired
by these amazing people

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.400
and I'm excited by what the next
generation will achieve.

